resolved_issues.body:
First of all, let's look at one piece of code in [hasti.scala](https://github.com/ucb-bar/rocket-chip/blob/master/src/main/scala/junctions/hasti.scala#L280):

``` scala
  for (m <- 0 until nMasters) {
    // If the master is connected to a slave, the slave determines hready.
    // However, if no slave is connected, for progress report ready anyway, if:
    //   bad address (swallow request) OR idle (permit stupid masters to move FSM)
    val autoready = nowhereM(m) || masters(m).isIdle()
    val hready = Mux1H(unionGrantMS(m), slaves.map(_.hready ^ autoready)) ^ autoready
    masters(m).hready := hready
    // If we diverted a master, we need to absorb his address phase to replay later
    diversions(m).io.divert := (bubbleM(m) || blockedM(m)) && NSeq(m) && hready
  }
```

When the number of masters is N and slaves is only one,  `unionGrantMS(m)` has only one element, the same as `slaves.map(_.hready ^ autoready)` . As the result, two parameters of `Mux1H` both are the vector that contains only one element.

Then let's look at the implement of `Mux1H` in [Mux.scala](https://github.com/ucb-bar/chisel3/blob/master/src/main/scala/chisel3/util/Mux.scala#L16):

``` scala
/** Builds a Mux tree out of the input signal vector using a one hot encoded
  * select signal. Returns the output of the Mux tree.
  *
  * @note results undefined if multiple select signals are simultaneously high
  */
object Mux1H {
  def apply[T <: Data](sel: Seq[Bool], in: Seq[T]): T =
    apply(sel zip in)
  def apply[T <: Data](in: Iterable[(Bool, T)]): T = SeqUtils.oneHotMux(in)
  def apply[T <: Data](sel: UInt, in: Seq[T]): T =
    apply((0 until in.size).map(sel(_)), in)
  def apply(sel: UInt, in: UInt): Bool = (sel & in).orR
}
```

Mux1H will pass his two parameters as a vector of tuple, witch contains only one tuple, to [SeqUtils.oneHotMux](https://github.com/ucb-bar/chisel3/blob/master/chiselFrontend/src/main/scala/chisel3/core/SeqUtils.scala#L54) : 

``` scala
  /** Returns the data value corresponding to the lone true predicate.
    *
    * @note assumes exactly one true predicate, results undefined otherwise
    */
  def oneHotMux[T <: Data](in: Iterable[(Bool, T)]): T = macro CompileOptionsTransform.inArg

  def do_oneHotMux[T <: Data](in: Iterable[(Bool, T)])(implicit sourceInfo: SourceInfo, compileOptions: CompileOptions): T = {
    if (in.tail.isEmpty) {
      in.head._2
    } else {
      val masked = for ((s, i) <- in) yield Mux(s, i.asUInt, 0.U)
      val output = cloneSupertype(in.toSeq map {_._2}, "oneHotMux")
      output.fromBits(masked.reduceLeft(_|_))
    }
  }
```

Because `in` contains only one element, `in.tail.isEmpty` is true and then it returns the second element of the tuple. **In other words, if input signal vector contains only one element, Mux1H will always see it as true and return the second parameter.**

Take back to `val hready = Mux1H(unionGrantMS(m), slaves.map(_.hready ^ autoready)) ^ autoready`, **all the masters will ready at the same time if the only one slave ready.**
 
Is this a bug of Mux1H? If not, what the situation is when` if (in.tail.isEmpty) { in.head._2 }`

